00:10:40.879 --> 00:10:44.391
This is the electrophysiology rig

00:10:44.391 --> 00:10:49.391
where electrophysiologist
can trace the signals

00:10:51.424 --> 00:10:54.943
in one in neuron.

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:59.600
And this is actually
not, it's very common,

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:02.640
and it's very much in use still

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:07.420
but there are much newer
techniques that can trace activity

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:10.160
of many neurons at one time.

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:13.850
And these machines look
a little bit different.

00:11:13.850 --> 00:11:16.330
We used to have two electrophysiologist

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:20.273
so that's another electrophysiology rig.

00:11:21.478 --> 00:11:26.478
And this is yet another room.

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:32.070
This here is cryostat
where we cut frozen brains.

00:11:33.430 --> 00:11:38.420
And then to either
immunohistochemistry or in C2

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:43.420
to detect different proteins
and RNase in brain tissue.

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:49.318
This machine is actually really cool.

00:11:49.318 --> 00:11:54.318
You can isolate fluid from the brain

00:11:54.714 --> 00:11:59.714
and then detect different type
of ions using this machine

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:05.930
and not only ions, but also
different neurotransmitters,

00:12:05.930 --> 00:12:09.640
it's micro dialysis machine.

00:12:09.640 --> 00:12:13.054
And this is confocal microscope.

00:12:13.054 --> 00:12:15.150
The confocal microscope

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:20.150
can detect different fluorescent light.

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:22.840
And I think you can detect

00:12:22.840 --> 00:12:25.727
as many as like six, eight at one time.

00:12:25.727 --> 00:12:29.160
We usually do not more than four,

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:31.330
which means that on one brain section,

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:34.120
you can detect multiple proteins

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:36.818
if you use the antibodies that are tagged

00:12:36.818 --> 00:12:40.338
with different fluorescent colors,

00:12:40.338 --> 00:12:45.338
and you can do four or more at one time.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:52.640
So we use all these
machines to study the brain

00:12:52.640 --> 00:12:57.640
and the lab studies
three different diseases,

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:02.210
depression, Alzheimer's,
and Parkinson's disease.

00:13:02.210 --> 00:13:07.210
And when I joined the lab, I
was actually kind of pushed

00:13:08.836 --> 00:13:12.070
politely towards the Alzheimer's disease.

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:15.100
And I was thinking to myself, oh my God,

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:19.167
it's like you have to wait
for a year, year and a half

00:13:19.167 --> 00:13:22.230
for things to finish,

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:24.850
and you can actually do your experiment.

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:25.810
And that was too long.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:27.030
I was too impatient.

00:13:27.030 --> 00:13:31.270
And I said, I'm gonna do more to disorders

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:34.830
because it's a much quicker result.

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:39.830
And I am actually now
having a really big project

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:42.010
on Alzheimer's disease.

00:13:42.010 --> 00:13:44.070
And it's very interesting,

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:48.400
but for a while I've
been studying depression.

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:51.200
And the way that we approach that easily

00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:56.200
we always either go top to
bottom or bottom to top,

00:13:56.712 --> 00:14:01.677
and the bottom here being the genes,

00:14:01.677 --> 00:14:06.677
and the top the behavior.

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:10.632
So there were a lot of questions here

00:14:10.632 --> 00:14:15.632
about why we have chosen
the mouse as a model.

