00:14:42.840 --> 00:14:46.203
So a lot of questions were about mice

00:14:46.203 --> 00:14:50.690
and how similar are they to people,

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:54.123
and why did we choose mice?

00:14:55.210 --> 00:15:00.210
A lot of it has to do with
being practical, right?

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:02.750
Yes, I understand the mouse.

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:06.847
Remember the thing that I
sent you, the little images?

00:15:08.976 --> 00:15:12.210
And the mouse brain looks really smooth

00:15:12.210 --> 00:15:15.530
and it's way smaller than the human brain.

00:15:15.530 --> 00:15:18.338
But I have to tell you that
everything that human brain has

00:15:18.338 --> 00:15:22.723
or let's say majority of
things that human brain has,

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:25.180
mouse brain has too.

00:15:25.180 --> 00:15:28.820
It's just packed in much smaller space.

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:30.022
The cells are smaller.

00:15:30.022 --> 00:15:32.570
The brain regions are smaller

00:15:32.570 --> 00:15:37.210
but basic circuitry and
how neurons look like,

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:41.350
and how they're connected
together is basically the same.

00:15:41.350 --> 00:15:46.350
Also the mechanism of how the
nerve impulse is generated.

00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:50.110
It's also the same.

00:15:50.110 --> 00:15:51.990
The speed might be different.

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:56.890
The external stimuli, the
response to environment

00:15:56.890 --> 00:15:59.270
might be slightly different,

00:15:59.270 --> 00:16:02.970
but in general, all of that
is more or less the same.

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:07.222
So that's why it is
perfectly fine to study

00:16:07.222 --> 00:16:11.300
all sorts of different
diseases on mice first.

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:16.300
And then if you found a
different drug therapy target

00:16:16.540 --> 00:16:19.480
or a different system or a gene

00:16:19.480 --> 00:16:21.770
that is responsible for a disease

00:16:21.770 --> 00:16:26.770
you can actually scale it
up to either bigger animals

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:31.170
which we don't really do that much anymore

00:16:31.170 --> 00:16:35.713
or do clinical studies on people.

00:16:37.230 --> 00:16:42.230
And there is also a few
other things about mice.

00:16:43.010 --> 00:16:46.702
First, they reproduce very fast

00:16:46.702 --> 00:16:49.270
and they develop very fast.

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:52.790
So the mouse is so-called adult

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:56.840
or rather young adult at six weeks.

00:16:56.840 --> 00:16:59.870
And that makes it very fast

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:04.870
in terms of getting more
mice and getting the results,

00:17:06.110 --> 00:17:09.250
the end result that you are looking for.

00:17:09.250 --> 00:17:11.622
And they're also small

00:17:11.622 --> 00:17:16.622
meaning they're cheap
to maintain in the lab.

00:17:16.870 --> 00:17:18.990
They don't need a lot of space.

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:20.970
They don't need a lot of food.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:25.462
And people who are
still working on monkeys

00:17:25.462 --> 00:17:29.440
always say that it's much
better to study monkeys,

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:33.750
but I personally would not
be able to do any experiments

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:36.802
on monkeys because they're
such amazing animals,

00:17:36.802 --> 00:17:40.940
and they remind me so much of little kids.

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:42.900
We actually have a
person that's Rockefeller

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:44.201
who works in monkeys,

00:17:44.201 --> 00:17:47.462
and we can see them in
the animal facility,

00:17:47.462 --> 00:17:49.638
and they're so much fun.

00:17:49.638 --> 00:17:50.793
I don't know.

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:54.997
When you pass by the
door they look at you,

00:17:54.997 --> 00:17:57.970
and they try to, I don't know.

00:17:57.970 --> 00:17:59.920
They make some chirping sound.

00:17:59.920 --> 00:18:03.480
It almost sounds like a bird chirping,

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:05.260
and they're really cute.

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:08.060
So I wouldn't be able to
do anything on monkeys.

00:18:08.060 --> 00:18:13.060
And I think that there is also a law now

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:18.400
that forbid use of, let's say
chimps for animal research

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:26.080
unless, I think in some special
circumstances such as COVID.

00:18:27.860 --> 00:18:29.980
Because I think that they tested

00:18:29.980 --> 00:18:32.070
the first vaccines on chimps

00:18:32.070 --> 00:18:33.660
although I'm not 100% sure.

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:37.110
But for that reason you
can still use chimps

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:42.110
which are 98% overlap,
gene overlap with people.

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:45.595
So they are incredibly similar to us.

00:18:45.595 --> 00:18:48.820
But other than that, I don't really think

00:18:48.820 --> 00:18:53.440
that a lot of neuroscience
is done on monkeys.

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:57.033
At least not neuroscience that I do.

00:18:57.033 --> 00:19:02.033
And so there was also a question.

00:19:05.334 --> 00:19:10.173
How do we approach depression in mice?

00:19:11.418 --> 00:19:14.370
When you look at them and their behavior

00:19:15.477 --> 00:19:17.310
you can actually see

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:22.310
that they exhibit similar behavior to us.

00:19:22.630 --> 00:19:25.570
It's just that they can't talk, right.

00:19:25.570 --> 00:19:26.970
But they're anxious.

00:19:26.970 --> 00:19:29.330
You can clearly see that they are anxious.

00:19:29.330 --> 00:19:30.547
If they are withdrawn

00:19:30.547 --> 00:19:33.587
you can clearly see
that they are withdrawn.

00:19:33.587 --> 00:19:38.587
If they are scared, you can
clearly see that they're scared

00:19:39.164 --> 00:19:41.520
but this is science, right?

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:42.910
So we have to measure everything.

00:19:42.910 --> 00:19:46.100
So there are a lot of behavioral tests

00:19:46.100 --> 00:19:51.100
that are designed to
measure certain behaviors.

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:54.868
So for depression,

00:19:54.868 --> 00:19:58.967
you know probably from, I
dunno just reading newspapers,

00:19:58.967 --> 00:20:03.209
that there are the many
different types of symptoms

00:20:03.209 --> 00:20:08.209
and each person doesn't
have all of the symptoms,

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:11.600
same as is with mice.

00:20:11.600 --> 00:20:14.700
And somebody asked me

00:20:14.700 --> 00:20:18.890
if there was something very
interesting that I found.

00:20:18.890 --> 00:20:21.767
So for me, the very
interesting thing that I found

00:20:21.767 --> 00:20:24.826
studying depression was
that you actually have mice

00:20:24.826 --> 00:20:27.173
who are naturally depressed.

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:31.790
So when we get, let's say 40 mice

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:33.962
and you do a behavioral test

00:20:33.962 --> 00:20:36.693
which is called social interaction,

00:20:38.003 --> 00:20:40.500
mice are actually social animals.

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:42.650
So they like to interact with each other.

00:20:42.650 --> 00:20:45.330
And they like to, it's same as with dogs.

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:47.830
When they see unknown dog on the street

00:20:47.830 --> 00:20:49.270
they immediately run to it

00:20:49.270 --> 00:20:52.000
and they sniff around
each other and so on.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:54.130
So the mice do the same.

00:20:54.130 --> 00:20:57.590
And you have this test
where you test the mice

00:20:57.590 --> 00:21:01.322
with unknown possibly aggressive mice,

00:21:01.322 --> 00:21:04.500
because they're way bigger than the mouse

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:06.280
that we are testing.

00:21:06.280 --> 00:21:11.280
If they sniff around, and the
mouse is also in like a cage.

00:21:12.641 --> 00:21:16.080
So it can't attack the other mouse.

00:21:16.080 --> 00:21:21.080
If the mouse that we are testing
is spending a lot of time

00:21:21.670 --> 00:21:24.480
around that unknown mouse,

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:27.870
we say that that's a normal behavior

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:30.260
because that's what they usually do,

00:21:30.260 --> 00:21:33.910
and that, that mouse has a
normal social interaction.

00:21:33.910 --> 00:21:38.490
However, you see mice
that are really super shy

00:21:38.490 --> 00:21:41.178
and don't spend any time
around the other mouse.

00:21:41.178 --> 00:21:46.178
So we always call them socially withdrawn,

00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:51.423
which is one of the
symptoms of depression.

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:54.040
Tracy, what do you think?

00:21:54.040 --> 00:21:56.420
Can I show them the, do we have time

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:58.740
for me to show them the actual test?

00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:00.433
I have a movie, yes?

00:22:00.433 --> 00:22:01.591
- Yes.
- Okay.

00:22:01.591 --> 00:22:03.293
- We have until about 10 o'clock.

00:22:04.290 --> 00:22:07.713
- So share screen again.

00:22:17.305 --> 00:22:20.730
This is a PowerPoint, actually. (laughs)

00:22:20.730 --> 00:22:21.563
- It's okay.

00:22:21.563 --> 00:22:22.396
(indistinct)

00:22:22.396 --> 00:22:26.052
- Finally I ended up on PowerPoint again.

00:22:26.052 --> 00:22:27.514
- I just also wanna make sure

00:22:27.514 --> 00:22:29.810
that there's some time for students

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:31.923
to actually share questions.

00:22:33.250 --> 00:22:38.250
- Okay, so this is control
mouse, so-called normal,

00:22:39.150 --> 00:22:40.363
and then look at them.

00:22:42.237 --> 00:22:47.237
The unknown mouse is here in this tube.

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:50.090
Look how different they are.

00:22:50.090 --> 00:22:55.090
This one goes in the
corner and just sits there.

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:58.770
Oops, I'm sorry.

00:22:58.770 --> 00:23:00.273
I don't know what happened.

00:23:03.019 --> 00:23:05.653
I did something strange.

00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:09.440
And so this is one of the tests

00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:14.440
that we use to detect depressive mice.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:16.193
You got the point.

00:23:16.193 --> 00:23:20.373
And then the mouse sat there
for another 10 minutes.

00:23:22.380 --> 00:23:27.380
So we then look at the
genes in different cells

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:34.259
and try to figure out how each gene

00:23:34.259 --> 00:23:38.723
that we find interesting
using bioinformatics methods.

00:23:39.578 --> 00:23:44.010
We try to change the
expression of that gene

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:47.060
and see how that affects behavior again.

00:23:47.060 --> 00:23:52.060
So we go again to the
behavior of the whole animal,

00:23:54.071 --> 00:23:59.071
and what we found was
that, I mean, not only us

00:24:03.430 --> 00:24:07.960
but other people too,
that depression actually

00:24:07.960 --> 00:24:11.590
is something called polygenic disease.

00:24:11.590 --> 00:24:15.920
So there is not one gene
that determines depression.

00:24:17.270 --> 00:24:20.910
There is not one gene that
drives the Alzheimer's

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:22.380
or Parkinson's disease.

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:25.520
There are many genes, and
that's why it's incredibly hard

00:24:25.520 --> 00:24:30.109
to A, find the reason of why
we develop these diseases,

00:24:30.109 --> 00:24:34.140
and B, to find a really successful therapy

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:37.810
that can help very fast.

00:24:37.810 --> 00:24:39.900
And for Parkinson's and Alzheimer's

00:24:39.900 --> 00:24:41.790
we still didn't find any therapy.

00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:46.383
So we are really trying
our best to work on that.

00:24:47.270 --> 00:24:51.490
And I don't know, do
you have any questions

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:55.090
because I think that I'm kind
of done with the project.

00:24:55.090 --> 00:24:59.040
I don't want to go to too much details

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:02.370
because I don't think
that this is the place

00:25:02.370 --> 00:25:05.200
and time for it.

00:25:05.200 --> 00:25:08.543
So do you have any other questions?

00:25:12.550 --> 00:25:15.870
I'm looking at your questions right now

00:25:15.870 --> 00:25:19.623
and see if I answered.

00:25:26.210 --> 00:25:28.826
How do you measure brain activity?

00:25:28.826 --> 00:25:33.212
So, as I said, brain activity
can be measured different ways

00:25:33.212 --> 00:25:35.500
both by gene expression,

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:38.800
but also we can do the the rig thing,

00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:42.790
the electrophysiology that I showed you.

00:25:42.790 --> 00:25:44.410
- We have a hand up from Zoe.

00:25:44.410 --> 00:25:46.610
So Zoe, I don't know if
you wanna unmute yourself

00:25:46.610 --> 00:25:49.970
and go ahead and ask your question.

00:25:49.970 --> 00:25:51.830
- [Zoe] Yeah, so you were talking

00:25:51.830 --> 00:25:54.860
about how like you had the depressed mice,

00:25:54.860 --> 00:25:57.300
but how do you make sure
that the mice that you get

00:25:57.300 --> 00:25:59.766
and the mice that like
reproduce and make more mice

00:25:59.766 --> 00:26:01.270
are actually depressed?

00:26:01.270 --> 00:26:02.103
Like how do you control

00:26:02.103 --> 00:26:05.040
for the fact that they might
not exhibit that specific gene?

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:08.362
- Okay, so that's the thing
that I didn't mention.

00:26:08.362 --> 00:26:10.180
It's an excellent question

00:26:10.180 --> 00:26:15.180
because it put me back to the
beginning of the research.

00:26:15.522 --> 00:26:18.320
We actually modeled depression.

00:26:18.320 --> 00:26:20.021
There are two ways to do this,

00:26:20.021 --> 00:26:22.501
actually three ways to do it.

00:26:22.501 --> 00:26:25.750
One way is to search for mice

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:27.830
that exhibit depression naturally

00:26:27.830 --> 00:26:31.040
and then just inbreed them
over several generation

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:35.160
with other mice that also
show depressive phenotype.

00:26:35.160 --> 00:26:38.645
And that way you get
actually an inbred strain

00:26:38.645 --> 00:26:43.645
which exhibits some
symptoms of depression.

00:26:43.987 --> 00:26:45.867
We don't do that in the lab.

00:26:45.867 --> 00:26:48.830
We do two different approaches.

00:26:48.830 --> 00:26:52.466
One is to make a transgenic mouse

00:26:52.466 --> 00:26:57.325
which has genes that are changed.

00:26:57.325 --> 00:27:02.325
And we already know that
this gene affects depression,

00:27:02.710 --> 00:27:06.484
so we change the gene and
make a transgenic mouse,

00:27:06.484 --> 00:27:11.484
and that's called either
knock out or knock in mice.

00:27:12.120 --> 00:27:13.730
So there are genetic models

00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:17.660
but it's only one gene that it is changed.

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:19.044
And then the third thing

00:27:19.044 --> 00:27:22.420
which is what I do is we model depression.

00:27:22.420 --> 00:27:24.850
There are definitely many different types

00:27:24.850 --> 00:27:28.563
of models for depression.

00:27:28.563 --> 00:27:33.563
We usually expose so-called
normal wild-type mice

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:36.450
to some harsh things.

00:27:36.450 --> 00:27:39.297
And that's why I'm not
gonna show you any movies,

00:27:39.297 --> 00:27:41.290
such as social defeat

00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:46.280
where one aggressive mouse
attacks another mouse.

00:27:46.280 --> 00:27:50.710
And we repeat that day
after day for 10 days

00:27:50.710 --> 00:27:53.090
for about a few minutes each day.

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:58.090
And finally the mice
become pretty depressed.

00:27:59.430 --> 00:28:01.749
There is also something
called restraint stress

00:28:01.749 --> 00:28:04.280
where you put them in
a really small tubes.

00:28:04.280 --> 00:28:05.863
So they can't move.

00:28:07.247 --> 00:28:12.247
There is social isolation
test where you keep mice

00:28:13.754 --> 00:28:14.717
one per cage.

00:28:14.717 --> 00:28:16.730
As I said, there are social animals,

00:28:16.730 --> 00:28:20.290
so they like to be with other mice,

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:24.310
and being alone in the cage
makes them incredibly depressed.

00:28:24.310 --> 00:28:29.310
So this is how we take these mice then

00:28:29.590 --> 00:28:32.250
and study their gene expression.

00:28:32.250 --> 00:28:37.250
So we actually really do
know and confirm by tests

00:28:37.423 --> 00:28:41.640
that we are working on a depressive mouse.

00:28:41.640 --> 00:28:43.662
And then we also have a control group

00:28:43.662 --> 00:28:47.680
which are just normal, wild type mice

00:28:47.680 --> 00:28:52.573
that were not exposed to
this harsh environment.

00:28:54.290 --> 00:28:57.410
And we compare them between each other

00:28:57.410 --> 00:28:59.763
doing all sorts of different tests.

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:04.350
Does that answer your question?

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:15.480
Okay, so somebody actually
asked if we are ready to go

00:29:19.670 --> 00:29:24.670
from a mouse brain to a
human brain as a next step

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:27.210
in my project.

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:29.163
Unfortunately I am not.

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:32.880
Although in my project,
I found that Statins

00:29:33.930 --> 00:29:38.393
which are usually taken for cholesterol,

00:29:39.710 --> 00:29:41.835
actually have a really pretty good effect

00:29:41.835 --> 00:29:46.570
on one specific gene and
what it does in the brain

00:29:46.570 --> 00:29:48.030
in terms of depression.

00:29:48.030 --> 00:29:52.460
So what I would like to do in
the future is to study people

00:29:52.460 --> 00:29:53.293
who take Statins

00:29:54.469 --> 00:29:59.469
and then for either heart
disease or cholesterol

00:30:00.170 --> 00:30:04.020
and see how prevalent
these depressive phenotype

00:30:04.020 --> 00:30:09.020
in these people, and then see
that if maybe a different dose

00:30:11.740 --> 00:30:15.850
of Statins can be added
to other antidepressants

00:30:15.850 --> 00:30:19.028
to help people who have depression

00:30:19.028 --> 00:30:23.577
especially if it's connected
to a stressful life.

00:30:23.577 --> 00:30:26.920
So that was kind of
another interesting thing

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:28.340
that came out of my study.

00:30:28.340 --> 00:30:30.104
I can't publish that, obviously

00:30:30.104 --> 00:30:32.390
because I don't have any people's data

00:30:32.390 --> 00:30:35.744
but I think that would be a nice next step

00:30:35.744 --> 00:30:37.844
to actually really work

00:30:37.844 --> 00:30:41.553
towards helping people with depression.

00:30:43.123 --> 00:30:48.123
Okay, there are some other
really interesting questions

00:30:48.984 --> 00:30:53.665
about, not really
connected to my research,

00:30:53.665 --> 00:30:58.560
but more more of the life I've chosen.

00:30:58.560 --> 00:31:01.770
Why did I choose to be neuroscientist?

00:31:01.770 --> 00:31:03.330
And do I have any advice?

00:31:03.330 --> 00:31:04.720
So maybe I should answer

00:31:04.720 --> 00:31:07.280
and talk a little bit about that, right?

00:31:07.280 --> 00:31:12.280
So I basically never did
anything else, but neuroscience

00:31:13.544 --> 00:31:16.060
except my undergrad.

00:31:16.060 --> 00:31:18.110
My undergrad was molecular biology

00:31:18.110 --> 00:31:21.750
and only one part like maybe
one course was neuroscience.

00:31:21.750 --> 00:31:25.330
And it was actually not my
favorite course by all means.

00:31:25.330 --> 00:31:29.400
I didn't like the professor
either, unfortunately.

00:31:29.400 --> 00:31:32.545
And after I finished college

00:31:32.545 --> 00:31:35.736
I wanted to go to graduate
school immediately.

00:31:35.736 --> 00:31:39.305
And the only place where they actually,

00:31:39.305 --> 00:31:44.260
they had immediate opening
was the neuroscience program.

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:46.770
And I said, well, why not?

00:31:46.770 --> 00:31:50.680
I can give it a try and slowly over.

00:31:50.680 --> 00:31:54.860
Let's say like two years, I got into it,

00:31:54.860 --> 00:31:56.450
and then really fell in love with it.

00:31:56.450 --> 00:31:59.050
So it was not the love on first sight.

00:31:59.050 --> 00:32:03.680
And this is my, I think best advice

00:32:03.680 --> 00:32:05.880
for anybody try different things.

00:32:05.880 --> 00:32:07.967
If you don't like something,

00:32:07.967 --> 00:32:12.967
but you think that there is
a nice opportunity for you,

00:32:13.314 --> 00:32:16.060
just stick around a little while longer

00:32:16.060 --> 00:32:17.280
and see if you like it.

00:32:17.280 --> 00:32:20.760
Don't just drop things immediately.

00:32:20.760 --> 00:32:23.610
If you don't like it in
terms of science obviously,

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:27.720
I can't talk about
other other professions.

00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:32.720
And then the other thing that
is also incredibly important

00:32:33.370 --> 00:32:38.370
in science is that you
don't get results fast.

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:43.660
I mean it's very rarely that
things work from the first go.

00:32:43.660 --> 00:32:47.350
And you have to just kind
of be incredibly patient.

00:32:47.350 --> 00:32:51.370
And I think the endurance and perseverance

00:32:51.370 --> 00:32:54.030
are incredibly important
for the scientist.

00:32:54.030 --> 00:32:57.010
And one of my advisors also told me

00:32:57.010 --> 00:33:02.010
that to be a good
scientist you need actually

00:33:02.095 --> 00:33:07.095
to deal with backseats pretty well,

00:33:07.100 --> 00:33:11.060
because there are a lot
of them along the line

00:33:11.060 --> 00:33:13.990
but then the reward when
things actually work

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:18.990
when you get good
results are so rewarding.

00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:23.010
And so it makes you so happy

00:33:23.010 --> 00:33:26.230
that it's actually worth doing it.

00:33:26.230 --> 00:33:30.040
And in terms of neuroscience,

00:33:30.040 --> 00:33:33.520
I think it's actually really,
really nice field to go to

00:33:33.520 --> 00:33:35.539
because there are so many different things

00:33:35.539 --> 00:33:40.400
and you don't actually have
to study only the brain.

00:33:40.400 --> 00:33:43.060
There are other parts of neurosciences

00:33:43.060 --> 00:33:47.917
that are incredibly important
such as spinal cord injury.

00:33:50.550 --> 00:33:55.550
And I don't know, even
heart disease and gut

00:33:57.896 --> 00:34:01.870
is incredibly hot topic right now

00:34:01.870 --> 00:34:05.710
because all of them need
brain and nerve cells

00:34:05.710 --> 00:34:07.170
to function properly.

00:34:07.170 --> 00:34:10.650
So that connection between
the heart and the guts

00:34:12.486 --> 00:34:16.162
with the brain is not necessarily
hardcore neuroscience,

00:34:16.162 --> 00:34:20.303
but also incredibly important
and interesting field.

00:34:25.328 --> 00:34:29.290
Cancers of the brain
are pretty devastating.

00:34:29.290 --> 00:34:34.020
And a lot of them with one
exception of medulla blastoma

00:34:34.020 --> 00:34:39.020
are pretty deadly, and they
are not easily treated.

00:34:39.350 --> 00:34:44.078
So that's another really
interesting field to go to.

00:34:44.078 --> 00:34:49.078
And then there is always basic
science that you can work on.

00:34:52.020 --> 00:34:54.535
So I think that there
is a lot of potential

00:34:54.535 --> 00:34:59.535
in neuroscience field and also
connection with psychology,

00:35:00.500 --> 00:35:03.620
with economics but that's
actually not my cup of tea

00:35:03.620 --> 00:35:05.943
because I really never did any of that.

00:35:05.943 --> 00:35:09.739
And I find it difficult
to read these papers

00:35:09.739 --> 00:35:13.530
because I don't understand the fine point.

00:35:13.530 --> 00:35:15.050
I understand the abstract,

00:35:15.050 --> 00:35:17.200
and then after that, not so much,

00:35:17.200 --> 00:35:20.890
maybe the first paragraph
for the introduction,

00:35:20.890 --> 00:35:24.530
but other than that,
summary is the only thing

00:35:24.530 --> 00:35:25.363
that I understand.

00:35:25.363 --> 00:35:27.970
But that's actually really
kind of cool fields.

00:35:27.970 --> 00:35:32.283
So, and there was a person Cedic,

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:35.950
that asked me about
something very specific.

00:35:35.950 --> 00:35:40.950
So this is why I like to do
these kinds of presentations

00:35:42.370 --> 00:35:45.830
because all your questions
force me to actually rethink

00:35:45.830 --> 00:35:47.453
why am I doing things.

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:52.940
So he asked, or she, I am sorry.

00:35:52.940 --> 00:35:56.450
I actually, I'm not sure
if it's a she or he.

00:35:56.450 --> 00:35:59.750
What do I think about Neuralink?

00:35:59.750 --> 00:36:03.780
I have to admit, I
didn't know what that is.

00:36:03.780 --> 00:36:07.884
So I Googled it and I actually,

00:36:07.884 --> 00:36:12.884
the therapy itself it's
known for a really long time.

00:36:14.778 --> 00:36:17.337
This is I think, just
the company that does it.

00:36:17.337 --> 00:36:19.843
One of the companies that does it.

00:36:21.175 --> 00:36:24.725
You put the electrodes in
different brain regions,

00:36:24.725 --> 00:36:28.764
and by activating these electrodes,

00:36:28.764 --> 00:36:33.764
you can actually turn on and
off and manipulate things

00:36:34.643 --> 00:36:36.644
around the house

00:36:36.644 --> 00:36:40.350
that I guess have some
sort of Wi-Fi connection.

00:36:40.350 --> 00:36:42.760
So you will think about
turning on the light

00:36:42.760 --> 00:36:44.810
and the light turns on.

00:36:44.810 --> 00:36:47.970
And I honestly, it gave me creeps

00:36:47.970 --> 00:36:52.910
because it sounded like
a science fiction movie,

00:36:52.910 --> 00:36:55.583
and I immediately could see all sorts

00:36:55.583 --> 00:36:58.880
of bad things that can happen with it.

00:36:58.880 --> 00:37:00.100
But on the other hand

00:37:00.100 --> 00:37:03.299
it's been done for Parkinson's disease

00:37:03.299 --> 00:37:08.299
and treatment resistant
depression for quite a while now.

00:37:11.140 --> 00:37:16.140
They put a little electrodes
in for depression area 25,

00:37:16.430 --> 00:37:20.350
Brodmann area 25, which is
somewhere here, deep down,

00:37:20.350 --> 00:37:24.520
and then for the Parkinson's
disease in substantia nigra

00:37:24.520 --> 00:37:27.059
which is somewhere here deep down

00:37:27.059 --> 00:37:32.059
to basically activate that area

00:37:32.870 --> 00:37:37.310
because the activity of
neurons itself is not adequate

00:37:37.310 --> 00:37:42.303
to help people A, overcome
their depressive phase,

00:37:43.990 --> 00:37:45.813
and for Parkinson's to actually move

00:37:45.813 --> 00:37:49.840
and apparently it works.

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:52.360
I mean, for Parkinson's
I saw in the movies

00:37:52.360 --> 00:37:56.921
it's amazing when you turn on
the electrode in the brain,

00:37:56.921 --> 00:38:01.921
the person who was really rigid
and could not move at all,

00:38:03.560 --> 00:38:07.251
all of a sudden starts talking and moving.

00:38:07.251 --> 00:38:11.000
And it's amazing what it can do.

00:38:11.000 --> 00:38:13.750
So I think that in that sense,

00:38:13.750 --> 00:38:17.892
Neuralink is actually
really good but it would be,

00:38:17.892 --> 00:38:22.892
and it is really great for a
very limited type of disease,

00:38:26.050 --> 00:38:29.324
such as Parkinson's
disease, maybe depression.

00:38:29.324 --> 00:38:33.620
It would not probably
be able to help people

00:38:33.620 --> 00:38:37.140
with multiple sclerosis, Alzheimer's.

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:38.705
It would be actually great for people

00:38:38.705 --> 00:38:40.740
who have spinal cord injuries.

00:38:40.740 --> 00:38:45.470
So for example, people who are
paraplegic, and cannot move,

00:38:45.470 --> 00:38:47.960
I think that it would be
absolutely great for them

00:38:47.960 --> 00:38:50.903
because it could allow them to move again.

00:38:51.830 --> 00:38:53.160
Can you imagine that?

00:38:53.160 --> 00:38:54.840
It would be amazing.

00:38:54.840 --> 00:38:57.614
So I am all for it, but on the other hand,

00:38:57.614 --> 00:39:01.494
I'm a little uneasy because just,

00:39:01.494 --> 00:39:05.943
I don't know thinking about
you want to, I don't know,

00:39:07.210 --> 00:39:12.100
create somebody or turn on
something that you should not,

00:39:12.100 --> 00:39:14.450
and you're actually able doing that

00:39:14.450 --> 00:39:17.950
with a little electrode in
your brain gives me creeps.

00:39:17.950 --> 00:39:18.783
I don't know.

00:39:18.783 --> 00:39:19.953
What do you think?

00:39:21.330 --> 00:39:23.710
You can maybe discuss
that with Tracy, later?

00:39:23.710 --> 00:39:27.726
It's another topic,
it's ethics in science.

00:39:27.726 --> 00:39:29.480
And I think everybody

00:39:29.480 --> 00:39:33.087
has to have that kind of really frank talk

00:39:33.087 --> 00:39:38.087
at all different times in
your learning experience

00:39:39.010 --> 00:39:41.590
in school, in college, in graduate school.

00:39:41.590 --> 00:39:44.920
I think that we need more ethical classes.

00:39:44.920 --> 00:39:47.503
So we actually know what
we're doing and why.

00:39:51.047 --> 00:39:56.047
Okay, does anybody, did I
answer all of your questions

00:39:57.750 --> 00:39:59.173
more or less?

00:40:02.430 --> 00:40:06.810
Something really, what is
the most challenging part

00:40:06.810 --> 00:40:08.393
of my research?

00:40:09.530 --> 00:40:12.450
Writing the paper. (laughs)

00:40:12.450 --> 00:40:14.819
The (indistinct) we're
done doing right now.

00:40:14.819 --> 00:40:19.819
You really need to actually write well.

00:40:20.650 --> 00:40:24.160
So for all your budding scientists there,

00:40:24.160 --> 00:40:29.160
don't discard your literature
classes and reading books

00:40:30.490 --> 00:40:33.900
because in writing, because
you are going to need that

00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:35.140
no matter what.

00:40:35.140 --> 00:40:36.670
If you'll choose science,

00:40:36.670 --> 00:40:39.440
and you'll go to work
in any kind of science,

00:40:39.440 --> 00:40:43.360
you do need to actually
compose your thoughts

00:40:43.360 --> 00:40:47.470
and to be able to put that
on the paper successfully.

00:40:47.470 --> 00:40:51.235
So other people are
really eager to read it

00:40:51.235 --> 00:40:54.070
and understand what's the message

00:40:54.070 --> 00:40:56.600
that you're trying to convey.

00:40:56.600 --> 00:40:58.878
So for me that's hard.

00:40:58.878 --> 00:41:01.118
Not only because English
is my second language

00:41:01.118 --> 00:41:05.343
but I think that I have
difficulties writing in Serbian too.

00:41:06.450 --> 00:41:08.880
So that's hard.

00:41:08.880 --> 00:41:13.880
And the other hard thing
is when things don't work,

00:41:15.930 --> 00:41:17.990
then you have to
troubleshoot to figure out

00:41:17.990 --> 00:41:21.792
why are they not working
to actually maybe even

00:41:21.792 --> 00:41:25.993
because if you're not
getting a result, that's bad

00:41:25.993 --> 00:41:28.960
but if you are getting
the different results

00:41:30.130 --> 00:41:32.340
than what you expected,

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:34.784
you really have kind of switch very fast

00:41:34.784 --> 00:41:39.130
and have a different overview

00:41:39.130 --> 00:41:41.143
because you need to explain

00:41:41.143 --> 00:41:44.020
why did you get the different results.

00:41:44.020 --> 00:41:46.760
So you have to switch
back and forth between.

00:41:46.760 --> 00:41:49.850
You can't basically rely on the thing

00:41:49.850 --> 00:41:54.313
on your hypothesis as set in
stone, you have to be flexible.

00:41:55.400 --> 00:41:58.773
So that's also not always easy.

00:42:03.839 --> 00:42:06.256
And oh, I like this question.

00:42:07.290 --> 00:42:09.800
Was there ever a time when you got stuck

00:42:09.800 --> 00:42:11.418
and had to start all over again?

00:42:11.418 --> 00:42:15.300
Man, so many times. (laughs)

00:42:15.300 --> 00:42:18.430
And don't be, I don't know.

00:42:18.430 --> 00:42:20.930
I think that it happens to everybody,

00:42:20.930 --> 00:42:25.212
and not only in science,
but in other professions.

00:42:25.212 --> 00:42:29.583
You just have to be very persistent.

00:42:30.510 --> 00:42:35.510
And in my what, 30 years
of science research,

00:42:39.080 --> 00:42:40.350
I changed fields.

00:42:40.350 --> 00:42:42.020
I mean, it was always neuroscience,

00:42:42.020 --> 00:42:44.809
but I've changed the topic several times.

00:42:44.809 --> 00:42:49.809
And actually most of the time
was because I kind of thought

00:42:50.500 --> 00:42:55.500
that the new field would be
very interesting to study

00:42:56.100 --> 00:42:57.450
and to learn something new.

00:42:58.460 --> 00:43:00.407
So that's it.

00:43:06.620 --> 00:43:08.673
Just continue.

